{:edb/db-before-t 1484 :edb/db-after-t 1485 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534797 50 #inst "2026-10-08T07:13:21.280Z" 13194139534797 true] [17592186151967 1100 "A body of mass $3 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F$, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N})$.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =t^2- e^{t-4}$ meters per second.\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=4$ seconds." 13194139534797 false] [17592186151967 1100 "A body of mass $3 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}).$\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =t^2- e^{t-4}$ meters per second.\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=4$ seconds." 13194139534797 true] [17592186151967 1145 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{e}^{t-4}) \\\\  & =2t-{e}^{t-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=4 \\, \\textrm{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =2(4)-{e}^{4-4} \\\\  & =8-1 \\\\  & =7\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot (7) \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F| = |21| = 21 \\, \\textrm{N}.\n$$" 13194139534797 true] [17592186151967 1145 "According to Newton’s second law\n$F=ma$,\nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t)$, we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - e^{t - 4}) \\\\\n&= 2t - e^{t - 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at\n\n$$\nt=4 \\, \\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= 2(4) - e^{4 - 4} \\\\\n&= 8 - 1 \\\\\n&= 7m/s^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= ma \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot (7) \\\\\n&= 21\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}F & = & 21\\end{vmatrix} = 21 \\, \\textrm{N}\n$$" 13194139534797 false] [17592186151976 1100 "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F$, measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t) = e^{3t-6} + t^2$ meters. Find the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t$." 13194139534797 false] [17592186151976 1100 "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t) = e^{3t-6} + t^2$ meters. Find the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" 13194139534797 true] [17592186151976 1145 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t-6}+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{3t-6}+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{e}^{3t-6}+2t) \\\\  & =9{e}^{3t-6}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force acting on the body, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =2\\cdot (9{e}^{3t-6}+2) \\\\  & =18{e}^{3t-6}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534797 true] [17592186151976 1145 "According to Newton’s second law\n$F=ma$,\nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t)$, we differentiate the displacement $s(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\frac{ds}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{3t - 6} + t^{2}) \\\\\n&= 3e^{3t - 6} + 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t)$, we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(3e^{3t - 6} + 2t) \\\\\n&= 9e^{3t - 6} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force acting on the body, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= ma \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (9e^{3t - 6} + 2) \\\\\n&= 18e^{3t - 6} + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534797 false] [17592186151977 1100 "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ moves in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F$, measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\geq 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =2t^3-12t^2-30t+5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time when the force acting on the body has a magnitude of zero." 13194139534797 false] [17592186151977 1100 "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ moves in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\geq 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =2t^3-12t^2-30t+5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time when the force acting on the body has a magnitude of zero." 13194139534797 true] [17592186151977 1145 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}-30t+5) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-24t-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-24t-30 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-4t-5 & =0 \\\\ (t+1)(t-5) & =0 \\\\ t & =-1,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t \\geq 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude when $t=5 \\, \\textrm{s}.$" 13194139534797 true] [17592186151977 1145 "According to Newton’s second law\n$F=ma$,\nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t)$, we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(2t^{3} - 12t^{2} - 30t + 5) \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} - 24t - 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6t^{2} - 24t - 30 &= 0 \\\\\nt^{2} - 4t - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(t + 1)(t - 5) &= 0 \\\\\nt &= -1, 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nt \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe disregard the negative solution.\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude when\n\n$$\nt=5 \\, \\textrm{s}\n$$" 13194139534797 false] [17592186152155 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310648 13194139534797 true] [17592186152155 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310655 13194139534797 true] [17592186152155 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310704 13194139534797 true] [17592186152155 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310708 13194139534797 true] [17592186152155 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310711 13194139534797 true] [17592186152164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310662 13194139534797 true] [17592186152164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310669 13194139534797 true] [17592186152164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310676 13194139534797 true] [17592186152164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310714 13194139534797 true] [17592186152164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310717 13194139534797 true] [17592186152165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310683 13194139534797 true] [17592186152165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310690 13194139534797 true] [17592186152165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310697 13194139534797 true] [17592186152354 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534797 true] [17592186152354 1100 